= Generalization (machine learning) =

== Overview ==
In machine learning, '''generalization''' is the ability of a learned system to achieve useful performance on new examples or conditions beyond the specific examples used to fit it.

Classical generalization usually concerns unseen samples drawn from the same or intended target data-generating process.

== Training and target performance ==
A model can fit its [[Training set|training set]] extremely well but generalize poorly.

Generalization is therefore evaluated on held-out or otherwise independent data whose outcomes were not used to fit the model.

== Generalization gap ==
A generalization gap is the difference between performance on training data and performance on new target data.

The exact quantity depends on the loss, metric, and sampling assumptions.

A small gap alone does not guarantee good performance: both training and test performance can be poor.

== Distribution assumptions ==
Classical statistical-learning results often assume training and test examples are independent samples from the same distribution.

[[Distribution shift]] weakens or breaks that assumption.

Performance under shift is often called out-of-distribution robustness or domain generalization and is a stronger/different problem than ordinary in-distribution generalization.

== Memorization ==
Memorizing training examples does not automatically imply failure to generalize.

Modern high-capacity systems can memorize some examples while still learning patterns that generalize.

Therefore:

<pre>
generalization ≠ absence of memorization
</pre>

== Related concepts ==
[[Overfitting]], [[Underfitting]], [[Inductive bias]], [[Bias–variance tradeoff]], [[Test set]], [[Distribution shift]], [[Spurious correlation]], and [[Shortcut learning]] explain different limits on generalization.

[[Robustness]] asks whether specified performance or function survives defined perturbations and is therefore related to, but broader/differently scoped from, ordinary in-distribution generalization.

